From 52f56dda571f8b9e0b202db6399ba3fbf2c679a3 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Andre Watson <dre@ligandal.com>
Date: Wed, 30 Sep 2026 20:24:46 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 35/38] fix(qsa): chunk-prefill reads K/V from the pool instead
 of gathering the whole context

forward_extend's prefixed (chunked-prefill) path index_select'ed and torch.cat'ed the
FULL sequence K and V of every request, per full-attention layer, per prefill chunk:
4 x seq_len x 512 B transient at fp8 (746.7 MiB at 382K, 1024 MiB at 512K; 2x at bf16).
Peak non-pool VRAM therefore scaled with context, not --chunked-prefill-size, and the
2026-09-30 16:44:17 GPU0 lane death OOM'd in exactly this torch.cat (:1470).

Now the top-k logical positions are mapped to pool slots by a small Triton kernel
(qsa_logical_to_pool_slots_triton) and the unchanged chunk-prefill kernel runs with
cu_k = 0 against the per-layer pool buffer. It loads the same bytes in the same order,
so the output is bit-identical (verified on RTX PRO 6000 at 64K..512K, fp8 and bf16,
mixed batches, -1-padded rows). Transient is the [rows, topk] slot table: 32 MiB at
chunk 4096, independent of context; the kernel is also 1.1-1.6x faster (no gather).
Slots are emitted as int64 once pool_rows x row_stride >= 2**31 (the kernel computes
slot * stride in the index dtype).

Host-resident (SGLANG_QSA_HOST_KV, UVA) pools are detected via pool.qsa_host_kv and
stage only the UNION of selected rows onto the GPU first: reading UVA in place is
~30x slower, and the union is never larger than the old full-context gather.
